WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:06.350
TRACY FREY: Hi, everybody.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.150
Welcome to "Responsible AI--

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.450
From Theory to Practice."

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.010
my name is Tracy Frey.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:15.370
And I'm the Director of
Product Strategy and Operations

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.060
for Cloud AI and
Industry Solutions.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.630
To start us off, I want to
root us all in this statement.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:26.290
In less than 10 years,
AI will be the number one

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:30.430
driver of global GDP growth.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:36.880
This graph shows that growth
through 2030, $13 trillion.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.220
And not only that,
organizations that

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.220
achieve AI absorption
will be the leaders

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:46.090
of the global
economy in that time.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.550
That growth has already started.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:52.090
People around the world have
become everyday AI users.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.880
And soon, technology
that isn't AI-enabled

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.070
will feel like
something is missing.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.220
Think of technologies that
we didn't have 10 years ago--

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.750
direct payment applications like
Venmo, virtual home assistance.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.780
Our reliance on AI
has already begun.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:11.950
And it will only
become more pronounced.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.280
Prior to 2012, AI
results closely

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.860
tracked Moore's law,
with compute doubling

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.600
every two years.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:26.320
Post-2012, compute has been
doubling every 3.4 months.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.460
For example, vision AI has
gotten more accurate and more

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:31.450
powerful.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.370
Take a look at the
error rate progression

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.000
here from 2011 to 2020.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.660
While this is undoubtedly
good news for innovation,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:45.580
it also requires more care and
attention from a responsibility

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.060
standpoint.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.500
A more accurate and powerful
vision AI technology,

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.320
when used in a
harmful way, can lead

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.400
to harmful and intentional
misuse, unintentional failure

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.090
modes, loss of personal
privacy, contributing

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:05.320
to severe real life
consequences for individuals,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.900
all at incredible
speed and scale.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.120
CCS Insights reports
that trusting AI systems,

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.260
just like the vision AI, is the
biggest barrier to adoption,

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.110
moving from the lab to
production for enterprises.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.440
There is a perception
that there is broadly

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.530
a deficit in the areas
of ethical diligence

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.840
over these powerful
technologies.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.050
We hear a lot of questions
on Responsible AI

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.330
once organizations
start to think

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.110
about moving to production.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.790
And Responsible AI is
impacting adoption.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.370
And as a team, we are seeing
more and more companies

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.830
come to us asking
for help because they

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.530
are facing challenges.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:53.450
There is a lack of trust in
the AI systems themselves.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:57.880
And when ethical issues arise,
which 90% of organizations

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.120
have encountered,
40% of companies

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.420
abandon instead of
solving for those issues.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.650
These numbers show a huge risk
in not taking responsibility

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.580
into consideration.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.820
It's not possible
for any technology

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.580
to be morally or values-neutral.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:20.380
Values are interpolated at
every stage of AI development.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.230
And each technology
is developed in order

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:27.430
to do something, which
expresses a value decision.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:31.000
That reveals a huge
barrier to AI adoption.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.450
And it's a key driver.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:38.260
One study found these
reasons for ethical issues

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:39.910
within organizations.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:45.280
Governance is reported to
be the biggest enterprise

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.980
requirement in AI in 2020.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.920
Not only will enterprises
embrace governance,

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.230
we can expect
increasing legislation,

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.240
potential regulatory issues.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:59.050
And consumers will begin to
judge and select organizations

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.360
based on these policies.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.370
Gartner reports
that, as consumers

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.550
become more aware and savvy
about how organizations are

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.700
using their data, and
organizations are using

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:14.140
increasing amounts of AI and
ML to drive business decisions,

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:18.130
enterprises must embrace
ideas like explainable AI

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:21.970
and transparent data policies
for both ethical and business

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.080
reasons.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:27.250
Customers will begin to judge
and select organizations

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.380
based on these policies.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:32.140
By 2020, Gartner
expects companies

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:34.390
that are digitally
trustworthy will

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:39.510
generate 20% more online
profit than those who are not.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:45.250
This all fits into
our belief at Google

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.540
that responsible AI
equals successful AI.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.880
We recognize that
the decisions we make

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:53.680
and the way we
develop technology

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.430
will have a lasting impact
The efficiency gains

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:01.630
and competitive advantage are
only going to be fully realized

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:04.690
in a world where AI is
responsibly developed

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.550
and trusted by end users.

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:10.040
If anywhere trust
gets broken, we

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.370
will see guy
getting pulled back.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:17.360
So to us, responsibly AI
really leads to successful AI.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.640
To build responsible
AI into our DNA,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.450
we began asking ourselves
a few questions.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.440
This is rooted in the idea
that people and businesses

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.380
with the best of
intentions can potentially

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.850
cause unintended harm.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.180
And we need to
understand the risks

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:44.710
and build out processes
to avoid those outcomes.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.990
For us at Google,
as you might relate,

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.240
we feel an immense
responsibility

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.750
to develop systems to answer
these critical questions

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:56.420
and build in the proper
principles, practices,

00:05:56.420 --> 00:06:00.040
and programs in support of
responsible AI development,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:03.370
to ensure that AI we build
and enable for the world

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:04.900
is successful.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.460
While we've come a long way,
this work is never finished.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.260
It is far from perfected.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.590
And we approach this
work from a humble place.

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:17.200
We are constantly
iterating and assessing

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.990
our work and progress,
but don't pretend

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:22.390
to have all of the answers.

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:24.880
Our goal today is to
share with you how

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.910
we've approached building
responsibility in by design

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:31.930
into our products, but more
importantly our organization

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.740
to support the necessary
inquiry, deliberation,

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:40.480
and culture to build products
aligned with our values.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.430
We'll focus mainly
on Google Cloud

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:45.220
because that's where we
sit, and because it's

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:49.400
arguably one of the more
complex areas to align.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:54.310
We have a range of general
purpose tools like auto-MLs

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:57.220
and APIs, all the
way to solutions

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.210
that go much farther up the
stack, like Contact Center AI.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:03.850
The way these
technologies can be used

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.340
can be quite varied
and hard to predict,

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.880
which makes it a good place
to discuss putting principles

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.780
into practice.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:16.330
What grounds our approach
to responsible AI at Google

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:18.690
are our AI principles.

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:23.310
Ethical AI, responsible AI,
and all of the associated terms

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.190
can feel amorphous or nebulous.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:31.200
How do you begin to wrangle
all of the often contradictory

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.790
ethical theories and critical
components of AI responsibility

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:39.990
into a systematic way to apply
them to your development?

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:43.380
For us, as for many
organizations now,

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.500
we found that the most
important first step

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.160
was developing our
company-wide AI principles

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.700
in the summer of 2017.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:58.610
So in 2018, we published
our AI principles.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:01.720
These are not just a
marketing campaign for us.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:05.920
They are the constitution we
use to build our AI products,

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:09.530
conduct research, and
draft our policies.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:12.010
Our set of AI
principles has been

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:15.730
one of the most important
things we have implemented.

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:19.200
They keep us motivated
by a common purpose--

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.520
succinctly
communicate our values

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.400
in developing advanced
technology for the world

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.760
and ensure that the way we
use artificial intelligence

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.520
is in the best
interest of humanity.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.400
We believe that organizations
and communities flourish

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:37.870
best when, in addition to our
own personal ethical values,

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.480
there are also shared
ethical commitments

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.570
within the community
that each person can play

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:47.170
a part in fulfilling, so that
our work is more about doing

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:51.010
something good together, not
doing whatever we privately

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:54.640
think is best and just
assuming or hoping

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.480
that others' values and choices
will magically mirror our own.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:05.430
But as we know, a plan on
paper, like these principles

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:08.330
and practices, are
only effective if they

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:09.960
are operationalized.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.750
Looking at a list of
principles like ours

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:16.020
can be hard to incorporate
into our day-to-day work

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:20.280
as we deal with detailed
engagements of our products.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.840
What remains true is that our
AI principles aren't magic.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.960
They don't immediately
answer all of our questions

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:29.470
on how to move forward.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:33.960
They don't relinquish us from
having hard conversations.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:38.220
They are a starting point in
establishing what we stand for

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.220
and what we can agree
to build toward.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:44.700
The work that has ensued
beyond the publication

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:48.450
of the principles has been
to interpret and apply them

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:49.860
in practice.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.660
At the end of the
day, if something

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:54.870
goes wrong with the technology,
and you have principles

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:59.470
like ours, and you can't prove
what you did to prevent that,

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:01.340
then that is a terrible outcome.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:06.560
We realized very
quickly that, in order

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:08.930
to be true to the
principles, we needed

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.810
to develop internal
governance processes that

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:15.680
put our principles into practice
in a systematic and repeatable

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.360
way.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:20.660
This includes building
responsible AI governance

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:25.010
capacity in the form of product
and customer deal review

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:28.550
committees, educational
programs, a partner program,

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:32.840
and trainings in how we consult
and work with our customers.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:35.820
We'll touch on each of these.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:39.270
Beginning with Google's
AI review processes,

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:41.850
the original hope was
that the principles

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:43.410
would be a decision guide.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:46.860
But in reality, they
can't because the company

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:51.000
has such a wide variety
of the ways it uses AI.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:54.930
The reality is that you can
take any baseline technology,

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:58.080
and it can be beneficial
when used in one way

00:10:58.080 --> 00:10:59.940
and harmful in another.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:03.600
So governing AI is not
only about the technology,

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.560
but it's the combination
of the technology, the use

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:11.970
case, the training data, the
societal context in which it

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:15.780
operates, and how it's
deployed in production.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.860
There is no world where blanket
statements can hold water

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:22.440
if you're going to
align the principles.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:25.650
And it requires that
people in the organization

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:28.740
are committed to the complexity.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.650
So we've built a
Google-wide ecosystem

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:34.020
around how we operationalize.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.670
We have a central
Google function

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:37.740
that consists of
a senior executive

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:41.940
council, which is supported by
responsible innovation team.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:43.860
That team is
charted with helping

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:47.370
teams around Google
in every product area

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:52.790
learn to operationalize the
principles for themselves.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:54.770
When we in Cloud
initially thought

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:58.320
about how to begin putting our
AI principles into practice--

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.580
and this happened before the
principles were published--

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:06.470
our first instinct, like
many, is that all we had to do

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:10.700
was create a big decision tree
and a checklist for what's OK

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:13.700
and what's not based on the
principles we established.

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:16.280
Our AI principles are
intentionally broad.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:19.970
And Google is a let 1,000
flowers bloom kind of place.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:23.360
And we have so much breadth in
the various product offerings

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:26.630
that it would be impossible to
have a checklist or decision

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:30.220
tree at the level of detail
that everyone would agree with.

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:33.920
And I guarantee you that's
the case for you as well.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:37.100
Checklists feel
comforting, but in practice

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:40.550
have been ineffective at
addressing governance processes

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:43.460
for such nascent technologies.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:45.890
In order to provide
rigorous evaluations

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:50.450
but flexibly allow for the new
technologies to take shape,

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:52.880
in Google Cloud, we've
created two separate review

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.480
bodies that are connected
but purposely distinct.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:59.540
The first review body
covers early stage customer

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:02.180
engagements, where
we are engaging

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:04.640
in custom AI or
advanced technology

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.740
with a customer or partner.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:10.210
This group is a
decision making group.

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:12.920
And it is very high throughput.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.790
And its intention is to
very quickly give an answer

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:20.830
to the field team as to whether
a project can move forward,

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:24.560
is not aligned with principles
and won't move forward,

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:28.850
or if it will move forward
with some conditions.

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:32.450
We've seen a range of those
decisions over the 2+ years

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:35.190
that we've been doing this.

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:39.600
The next half of the process
is how we build and develop

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:40.710
our products.

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:43.980
When it comes to the AI
products we develop at Google,

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:48.300
we undertake rigorous and
deep ethical analysis, risk,

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.630
and opportunity assessments
across each principle.

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:53.700
And we create
alignment proposals

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:56.610
that we bring to those
reviews, where we often

00:13:56.610 --> 00:14:01.080
have very uncomfortable but
extraordinarily inspiring

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:02.850
conversations.

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:06.420
With a healthy product pipeline,
we aim for in-depth reviews

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:11.430
every two weeks relatively early
on in the product development

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:14.220
lifecycle, which is essential.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:17.280
In advance of the review
meeting, a member of the team

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.850
takes on the task of doing
an initial evaluation

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:23.400
of the product,
working hand-in-hand

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:26.430
with the technical teams
to understand the product

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:30.210
in-depth, in order to assess
the principles implications

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:32.700
and recommend
mitigations to build

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:34.860
into the plans for the product.

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:39.180
This document serves as the
basis for the discussion

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:40.740
come review time.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:45.450
This meeting of the minds is
one of the most important steps

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:49.890
in any principles evaluation
to allow us to do two things--

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:54.030
issue spot as a team, and
come to outcomes and paths

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:57.700
forward for products that
are not just good in theory,

00:14:57.700 --> 00:15:00.870
but are able to be
actualized in practice.

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.600
Over time, these
conversations have

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:06.570
been effective at
normalizing conversations

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:11.070
about risky technology and
potentially adverse outcomes

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:14.080
that we can then prevent.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:16.240
The next question, I'm
sure you're wondering,

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:19.060
is, what do those paths
forward look like?

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:23.030
The paths forward can look
like a lot of different things.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:25.270
It can be that we
more narrowly scope

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:27.250
the purpose of the technology.

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:30.070
In some cases, this can
mean we make a decision

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:33.730
not to launch a product
as a general purpose API

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:36.400
or without an allow list.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:39.370
As you may have seen,
we decided in 2018

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:41.380
that we wouldn't make
facial recognition

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:44.140
available as a
general purpose API.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:46.420
Even though the
technology exists,

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:49.660
we won't make it available
for anyone to use.

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:52.690
And we found a path
forward for the technology

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:56.560
by scoping it to a narrow
celebrity recognition offering,

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:59.500
which we'll talk about in a bit.

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:02.590
Other mitigations can
look like identifying

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:05.530
educational materials
or best practices

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:08.350
to package up with a
product and launch it.

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:11.050
This has taken the shape
of launching the product

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:14.650
with the associated model
card, a project we took on

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:18.220
to organize the essential facts
of a machine learning model

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:20.590
in a structured
way, or launching

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:24.430
more specific implementation
guides like our inclusive ML

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:27.550
guide embedded in
our product UIs.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:30.520
From there, we create
an alignment plan

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:33.430
with the mitigations discussed
in the meeting, which

00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:37.000
then goes through signoff by
our product and engineering

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.350
leadership.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:41.350
It's key to note that
not all paths forward

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:46.420
involve tech solutions or fixes
because ethics problems are not

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:49.690
always the result of
technology lapses,

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:54.310
but are actually more a
product of the societal context

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:59.710
in which it's being deployed
and how it's used as intended.

00:16:59.710 --> 00:17:02.710
Over time, through
this review process,

00:17:02.710 --> 00:17:08.260
we identify patterns similar to
case law, where we are creating

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:12.220
a growing set of precedents
and best practices

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:17.530
on sensitive topics to inform
decisions and drive consistency

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:21.250
on whether a new product
can go ahead and with what

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:22.589
mitigations.

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:26.619
There is no one size
fits all approach.

00:17:26.619 --> 00:17:29.450
Flexibility is important.

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:32.590
And it's not just
a point in time.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:35.600
As that societal
context changes,

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:38.470
then we have to
respond differently

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:42.340
as we make our
decisions going forward.

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:44.380
Here are some of the
imperatives and challenges

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:47.660
we've learned over
those past two years.

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:50.140
One thing that I
really want to stress

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:53.320
is the importance of a
diverse review committee.

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:56.020
Members are intentionally
multi-level.

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:59.170
We have folks who are just
starting their careers out

00:17:59.170 --> 00:18:02.050
of college all the
way up through VPs.

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:05.230
We're drawing on
a range of experts

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:08.290
from a diversity of
backgrounds, both technical and

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:09.550
non-technical.

00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:12.620
Multiple team members,
including myself,

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:15.160
have deep social
science, and philosophy,

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:17.470
and ethics backgrounds.

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:21.250
And we are really intending
to be cross-functional

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:26.220
across every role that exists
at Google, for the most part,

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:29.740
and that we are not just drawing
from people within Cloud,

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:35.020
but that we are drawing from
expertise across Google.

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:39.980
For each review, we also seek
out additional expertise,

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:43.210
whether that be on
issues of machine

00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:46.365
learning fairness and
robustness, or ethical AI,

00:18:46.365 --> 00:18:52.000
or a particular use case
or technology, or domain,

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:53.230
or industry.

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:55.450
We're really lucky
that, at Google, we

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:58.840
have a lot of that
expertise in-house.

00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:03.460
And when we don't have it in our
walls, we seek it externally.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:07.210
One of the other most important
things we did in the beginning

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:11.560
is we had the great
opportunity to hire a visiting

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:16.300
researcher, Shannon Fowler, who
was a tech ethicist and world

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:17.410
renowned.

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:21.940
And she worked with us to
help us build this process

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:24.970
and engage in our
reviews in a way that

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:30.010
was incredibly useful for us.

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:31.570
So what we've learned--

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:33.420
we've learned that
constitutions,

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:36.600
like our AI principles,
require interpretation.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:40.170
Predicting misuse helps
develop guardrails.

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:42.930
This requires us to
imagine what if and explore

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:46.140
all the possible scenarios
and areas of misuse

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:48.450
in order to develop
a comprehensive set

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:49.770
of guardrails.

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:51.420
Our principles aren't magic.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:53.400
They don't answer
the tough questions.

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:56.400
They're a framework for
those conversations.

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:59.590
Deliberation and healthy
disagreement is key.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:02.220
We do not hold our
team as an ivory tower

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:05.490
full of moral heroes, but
working collaboratively

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:08.910
within our technical teams
to service potential issues

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:11.310
and remediate them together.

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:14.460
Ethical AI is not only
about the extremes.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.690
And it's not always
about AI for social good.

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:19.140
And it shouldn't be.

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:24.150
Every AI product, regardless
of how innocuous it may seem,

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:27.030
should be put through
principles evaluations.

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:32.200
Here are some of the
trainings we offer Googlers

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:34.840
to build responsibly
AI into our culture

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:37.900
and equip teams with
the tools and knowledge

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:41.620
to insert responsible AI
into their day-to-day work.

00:20:41.620 --> 00:20:46.860
Some of these are available
externally as well.

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:50.130
Our aim is to connect
and collaborate

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.780
with partners
across the industry.

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:55.410
We want to share our
learnings, learn,

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:58.050
hear fresh and
varied perspectives,

00:20:58.050 --> 00:21:00.210
and advance our
collective thinking when

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:04.460
it comes to responsible AI.

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:08.200
The final way in which we put
our principles into practice

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:10.420
is through a growing
set of tools.

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:12.370
Responsible AI
tools and frameworks

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:15.670
are becoming an increasingly
important and effective way

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:19.610
to explain and
understand our AI models.

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:22.210
Tools get at the
questions, how do I

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:24.730
understand the
predictions made by AI?

00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:29.170
How do I know if my data,
or model, or outcomes

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:30.550
have unfair bias?

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:33.730
And how do I know
what is influencing

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:36.150
my model's predictions?

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:37.710
Building tools
and infrastructure

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:41.280
to evaluate and improve
concerns at scale--

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:43.320
today, we'll touch
on four tools we

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:46.980
offer that get at some of the
main concerns facing AI today--

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:50.580
fairness indicators,
what-if tool, model cards,

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:53.770
and explainable AI.

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:57.190
We often get asked
about debiasing models

00:21:57.190 --> 00:22:00.220
and how we Google think
about ML fairness.

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:03.790
Broadly speaking, there
is no standard definition

00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:05.920
of fairness, whether
decisions are being

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:08.830
made by humans or machines.

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:12.550
Identifying appropriate
fairness criteria for a system

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:16.930
requires accounting for user
experience, cultural, social,

00:22:16.930 --> 00:22:21.550
historical, political, legal,
and ethical considerations,

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:24.700
several of which
may have trade-offs.

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:29.290
I also want to stress that there
is no future in which we will

00:22:29.290 --> 00:22:33.850
be able to say, in my opinion,
that we have achieved fairness,

00:22:33.850 --> 00:22:37.790
that we can say we're done with
this work and we can move on.

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:41.920
This is something that we
engage in for the long term.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:45.310
And we are always learning
more and evolving.

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:49.020
And we'll show you some
examples of that in a bit.

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:51.170
So why does Google care
about these problems?

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.360
Well, our users are diverse.

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:55.370
And it's important
that we provide

00:22:55.370 --> 00:22:59.940
an experience that works equally
well across all of our users.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:01.670
The good news is that
we have the power

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:04.160
to approach these
problems differently

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:07.730
and to create technology
that is fairer and more

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:10.010
inclusive for more people.

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:12.290
We'll start with an
overview of what that means.

00:23:12.290 --> 00:23:14.330
And I should also
say that this is

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:18.740
an area we are still learning
about and always will be.

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:21.620
Addressing fairness in
AI is an active area

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:24.620
of research at Google
and beyond from fostering

00:23:24.620 --> 00:23:28.010
a diverse workforce that
embodies critical knowledge,

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:32.690
from training models to remove
incorrect problematic biases.

00:23:32.690 --> 00:23:36.320
Knowing that there is no
standard definition of fairness

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:40.100
presents both an
opportunity and a challenge.

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:43.870
One thing that I also think
is really important that we've

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:48.650
built into our review process
is a clear recognition

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:51.440
that we may not,
in our group, have

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:54.200
the appropriate lived
experience that can help

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:56.930
inform how we build a product.

00:23:56.930 --> 00:23:59.960
We'll show you some
examples of this in a bit.

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:02.660
But it's important
that we recognize--

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:06.860
and that I recognize and
stress, for our team, that we

00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:09.680
are coming from a place
of great privilege

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:12.870
and to honor that
as much as we can.

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:16.100
This requires creating
a space that is

00:24:16.100 --> 00:24:18.590
rich with psychological safety.

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:21.930
And I cannot stress enough the
importance of doing that work.

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:26.620
An important step
on this path is

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:29.260
acknowledging that humans are
at the center of technology

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:32.440
design, in addition to
being impacted by it.

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:36.190
And humans have not always
made product design decisions

00:24:36.190 --> 00:24:39.730
that are in line with
the needs of everyone.

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:44.740
Until 2011, automobile
manufacturers

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:48.430
were not required to use
crash test dummies that

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:51.100
represented female bodies.

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:57.250
As a result, women were 47% more
likely to be seriously injured

00:24:57.250 --> 00:24:59.080
in a car accident than men.

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:06.480
Bandaids have long been
manufactured in a single color,

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:09.520
a soft pink, my skin tone.

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:12.100
In this tweet, you see
the personal experience

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:15.430
of an individual using a
Bandaid that matches his skin

00:25:15.430 --> 00:25:17.750
tone for the first time.

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:19.420
I will never forget.

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:22.600
I had a third grade
assembly where somebody

00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:27.220
came, a father of one of
my classmates, and told us,

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:31.840
pointed out that Bandaids were
made for white skin tones.

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:35.320
So were Crayons,
so were hosiery.

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:37.930
That was what the
nude color was.

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:42.400
And once you know this,
you can't unsee it.

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:44.360
It changes you forever.

00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:48.460
And this is why surfacing
these issues is so important.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:52.030
A product that's designed and
intended for widespread use

00:25:52.030 --> 00:25:55.630
shouldn't fail for an individual
because of something they

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:57.790
can't change about themselves.

00:25:57.790 --> 00:26:01.240
Products and technology
should work for everyone.

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:07.300
The choices made in
those two examples

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:09.010
reinforce the
importance of being

00:26:09.010 --> 00:26:12.430
thoughtful about technology
design and the impact it

00:26:12.430 --> 00:26:14.380
might have on humans.

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:16.300
As in the case with
those two examples,

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:18.333
humans are at the center
of machine learning

00:26:18.333 --> 00:26:19.000
and development.

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:23.320
And fairness concerns can easily
become embedded into AI product

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:24.950
design decisions.

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:28.420
So how can we approach
technologies to make

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:31.490
them more inclusive and fair?

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:36.890
Unfairness can enter into the
system at any point in the ML

00:26:36.890 --> 00:26:38.010
pipeline--

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:41.900
here from data collection and
handling, to model training,

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:43.550
to end use.

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:46.220
In this typical view
of an ML pipeline,

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:49.950
you collect data, you label
the data, you train the model

00:26:49.950 --> 00:26:51.740
and using certain objectives.

00:26:51.740 --> 00:26:54.500
The data is packaged
and put into a product.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:57.320
And users see the
effect and engage.

00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:01.460
The way users engage informs
more data collection.

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:04.550
Within that pipeline,
the way we sample data,

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:07.520
the way we label it, how
the model is trained,

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:11.330
and whether or not the objective
leaves out a particular set

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:15.050
of users, how it was
packaged, and what kinds

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:19.280
of unconscious biases our
users bring engagement,

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:22.220
and how that affects,
how they behave,

00:27:22.220 --> 00:27:27.230
and thus how we collect more
data can all work together

00:27:27.230 --> 00:27:31.690
to create unfair biased systems.

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:36.580
Rarely can you identify a single
cause of or a single solution

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:38.050
to these problems.

00:27:38.050 --> 00:27:42.850
Far more often, various
causes interact in ML systems

00:27:42.850 --> 00:27:45.040
to produce problematic outcomes.

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:47.620
And a range of
solutions is needed.

00:27:47.620 --> 00:27:51.700
The work of ML fairness is to
disentangle these root causes

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:55.840
and interactions and
find ways forward.

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:59.020
What's important is getting
clear on the questions

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:00.230
we need to answer.

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:02.110
There are a smattering
of questions

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.690
that can be used to
guide your fairness

00:28:04.690 --> 00:28:07.480
work across the
pipeline, everything

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:11.500
from upfront product decision,
which is often under specified

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:13.000
in a lot of cases.

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:16.210
Really defining the
problem is all the way

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:21.010
to identifying if and when a
model isn't behaving correctly.

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:24.340
We want to know why
did the model fail.

00:28:24.340 --> 00:28:25.990
Is the model trustworthy?

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:28.090
What are the limitations?

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:31.180
We've found these questions
to be helpful in guiding

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:32.440
our investigations.

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:35.590
Underlying all of this is
making sure the workforce is

00:28:35.590 --> 00:28:36.610
inclusive.

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:41.440
The best way to make sure that
we, as teams, are equipped

00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:46.000
is to have a diverse workforce
having these conversations.

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:49.330
Back to our pipeline-- at
each stage of this lifecycle,

00:28:49.330 --> 00:28:53.230
we have identified tools,
best practices, and frameworks

00:28:53.230 --> 00:28:55.750
to help ML practitioners
in their efforts

00:28:55.750 --> 00:28:57.580
to build responsible AI.

00:28:57.580 --> 00:29:00.760
At the start, we are looking
at problem definitions

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:03.970
and the resources we
have when we initially

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:07.750
qualitatively speak to
what we want to accomplish.

00:29:07.750 --> 00:29:11.230
But when we talk about fairness,
some of the tools and resources

00:29:11.230 --> 00:29:14.410
we have found valuable
here for fairness

00:29:14.410 --> 00:29:18.190
are the stages at the
end of the ML lifecycle,

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:21.490
where we've really focused
in terms of tool development

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:26.285
and transparency frameworks in
the evaluation and monitoring

00:29:26.285 --> 00:29:26.785
stages.

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:33.410
I want to move now
into some case studies

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:38.690
to show you some examples of
how this work has gone for us

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:41.840
and what we've done both
in the process of launching

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:46.870
products and evaluating
problems when they arose.

00:29:46.870 --> 00:29:50.110
The first case study outlines
our approach in Cloud

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:54.250
to facial recognition and
how we got to this approach

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:56.980
through our principles
and the review

00:29:56.980 --> 00:30:02.330
processes we've put into
place to operationalize them.

00:30:02.330 --> 00:30:04.210
I'm going to start
with the outcome

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:07.390
of our in-depth review of
enterprise facial recognition

00:30:07.390 --> 00:30:08.440
technology.

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:10.840
You can see here the
headline is the launch

00:30:10.840 --> 00:30:14.740
of a tightly scoped API
called Celebrity Recognition,

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:18.910
available to professional media
and entertainment customers.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:21.040
We launched this last fall.

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:24.100
You may realize that
video is all but

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:27.940
unsearchable without expensive
and labor-intensive tagging

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:28.990
processes.

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:32.920
This makes it really difficult
for creators and platforms

00:30:32.920 --> 00:30:35.920
to organize their content
and cater to the increasing

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:38.920
demand for personalized
experiences,

00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:42.100
or even to really understand
what content they have.

00:30:42.100 --> 00:30:46.550
So Celebrity Recognition
is a pre-trained AI model,

00:30:46.550 --> 00:30:48.610
which means it's
not customizable,

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:52.810
that is able to spot thousands
of popular actors and athletes

00:30:52.810 --> 00:30:56.570
from around the world
based on licensed images.

00:30:56.570 --> 00:30:59.380
This is Cloud's first
enterprise product

00:30:59.380 --> 00:31:02.380
with facial recognition.

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:05.310
So I want to talk a
bit about the history.

00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:08.940
In early 2016, Cloud
leadership decided

00:31:08.940 --> 00:31:11.460
not to make facial
recognition available

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:15.090
as part of the Cloud
Vision API offering,

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:17.490
despite it being one
of the top requests

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:20.970
from customers coming out of
data which had started the year

00:31:20.970 --> 00:31:22.260
before.

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:26.220
In 2017, facial recognition
was identified internally

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:29.490
as a key concern for
potential unfair bias

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:33.030
in our definition of
algorithmic unfairness, which

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:35.550
was written by our
Privacy and Security,

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:38.100
and now Responsible
Innovation and Privacy

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:41.010
colleagues, the core
definitional document

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:44.850
for Google's machine
learning fairness efforts.

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:48.540
We decided to take face
recognition through the process

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:50.900
we described earlier.

00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:55.250
In mid-2020, just recently,
we welcomed the news

00:31:55.250 --> 00:31:58.940
that other technology companies
were limiting or exiting

00:31:58.940 --> 00:32:01.320
their facial
recognition business.

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:03.200
And this was thanks
significantly

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:07.070
to the gender sheets work,
which really informed

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:10.040
how this technology
can have impact

00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:16.340
across a range of unfair biases
from skin tone to gender.

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:21.740
As you know, we decided not to
make a generally available face

00:32:21.740 --> 00:32:26.600
recognition API available
because of our process

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:29.700
back in 2018.

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:31.970
So what we did,
initially within Cloud,

00:32:31.970 --> 00:32:36.230
is an in-depth review of face
recognition technology by both

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:39.050
our dealer view and our
product review bodies,

00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:42.410
as well as the central
Google executive council.

00:32:42.410 --> 00:32:44.660
Our own internal
review processes

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:48.920
necessitated a broad look at
the research, societal context,

00:32:48.920 --> 00:32:51.920
and complexities around
facial recognition.

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:55.760
Going through these processes--
and this was the second review

00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:56.990
we ever did--

00:32:56.990 --> 00:32:59.480
gave us the open
forum and the time

00:32:59.480 --> 00:33:02.390
to really think critically
about the challenges.

00:33:02.390 --> 00:33:05.930
And we realized that
alone we couldn't do this.

00:33:05.930 --> 00:33:08.150
So we sought help
from third parties

00:33:08.150 --> 00:33:11.840
to work with us on both external
benchmarking and performance.

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:15.260
And we began a human rights
due diligence process,

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:17.360
where we called
on domain experts,

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:20.150
and civil rights leaders,
and human rights leaders

00:33:20.150 --> 00:33:23.060
that would allow us to
incorporate the perspectives

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:24.980
of impacted people.

00:33:24.980 --> 00:33:29.210
Just a reminder of going back
to that conversation about how

00:33:29.210 --> 00:33:31.880
our lived experience
is not going

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:36.200
to help us inform the
lives and perspectives

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:37.400
of impacted people.

00:33:37.400 --> 00:33:40.070
And so it was really
important that we did the work

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:44.980
to understand that because
the reality is everything

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:49.400
we do, every product we
launch, every market we enter

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:52.150
does not exist in a vacuum.

00:33:52.150 --> 00:33:54.130
And in order for us
to make decisions

00:33:54.130 --> 00:33:56.200
that are aligned with
our AI principles,

00:33:56.200 --> 00:34:00.280
we have to take this into
account at every step.

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:04.150
Technical analysis
alone is not enough

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:06.070
because the reality
is that there

00:34:06.070 --> 00:34:10.810
is systematic societal
underrepresentation in media

00:34:10.810 --> 00:34:13.420
of Black and minoritized actors.

00:34:13.420 --> 00:34:18.420
And this needed to be the
basis for our evaluation.

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:20.940
As a side note, just
with the analysis

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:22.949
that the Geena
Davis Institute did

00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:26.250
on gender, which showed
that women-led films made

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:31.739
16% more in the box office,
despite men being both seen

00:34:31.739 --> 00:34:35.880
and heard twice as
much as women on film,

00:34:35.880 --> 00:34:39.389
you can see that films with
racially diverse co-leads

00:34:39.389 --> 00:34:44.730
had created 60% more in
gross income those films,

00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:47.969
but were grossly
underrepresented in film

00:34:47.969 --> 00:34:50.710
more broadly.

00:34:50.710 --> 00:34:53.230
Human rights due diligence
became a core part

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:54.730
of what we needed to do.

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:58.300
Human rights are a basic code
of conduct for all human beings.

00:34:58.300 --> 00:35:01.840
And we honor the universal
declaration of human rights

00:35:01.840 --> 00:35:02.665
through the UN.

00:35:02.665 --> 00:35:07.090
And so again, we really wanted
to seek external voices to help

00:35:07.090 --> 00:35:08.920
us make this assessment.

00:35:08.920 --> 00:35:12.490
And so we engaged in a process
called the Human Rights Impact

00:35:12.490 --> 00:35:13.490
Assessment.

00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:16.480
We did this through an
organization called BSR.

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:20.380
And BSR stands for Business
for Social Responsibility.

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:23.770
And they helped us to
really significantly improve

00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:25.570
our product offering.

00:35:25.570 --> 00:35:30.700
Engaging with BSR validated
many of our already planned

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:33.160
for alignment opportunities.

00:35:33.160 --> 00:35:37.780
And we incorporated almost all
of their additional suggestions

00:35:37.780 --> 00:35:40.630
across a range of
aspects of the product.

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:42.640
Their report is
publicly available.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:46.390
And I encourage all
of you to read it.

00:35:46.390 --> 00:35:48.220
All of this guidance
and the reviews

00:35:48.220 --> 00:35:51.400
informed where we needed to
do our testing and fairness

00:35:51.400 --> 00:35:52.480
analyses.

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:54.850
And it revealed to us
both where the solution

00:35:54.850 --> 00:35:58.570
needed additional oversight
and validated the decision

00:35:58.570 --> 00:36:02.750
not to offer a general purpose
facial recognition API.

00:36:02.750 --> 00:36:04.630
So in the coming
slides, I'm going

00:36:04.630 --> 00:36:08.080
to show you a deep dive into
the investigations that then

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:10.300
further informed the
product decisions we

00:36:10.300 --> 00:36:13.130
made before launching.

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:16.260
So over three separate
fairness tests,

00:36:16.260 --> 00:36:19.310
we found a discrepancy
in our training data

00:36:19.310 --> 00:36:23.270
sets falling on skin tone
lines in one of those tests.

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:27.000
It's the one on the
right, the Eastwood v2.

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:29.970
This was obviously
very upsetting.

00:36:29.970 --> 00:36:33.300
And just like we saw
earlier in the presentation,

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:37.140
for many organizations, this is
where they would have stopped

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:40.630
and said, we are just
not going to do this.

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:43.950
But because we knew that
we wanted to evaluate this

00:36:43.950 --> 00:36:48.740
as thoroughly as we could, we
decided to take a deeper look.

00:36:48.740 --> 00:36:51.050
The first thing we
had to determine

00:36:51.050 --> 00:36:53.900
was if the skin tone
labels were accurate.

00:36:53.900 --> 00:36:56.480
The answer was sort of.

00:36:56.480 --> 00:37:00.590
They were not terribly
accurate in categorizing medium

00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:02.270
and darker skinned people.

00:37:02.270 --> 00:37:05.360
So based on the seminal work
and the findings of the Gender

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:07.970
Shade Study, we
categorized skin tone

00:37:07.970 --> 00:37:12.050
into three buckets using the
Dermatological Fitzpatrick Skin

00:37:12.050 --> 00:37:16.220
Type Scale, which is an estimate
of skin sensitivity to UV

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:18.270
radiation.

00:37:18.270 --> 00:37:20.460
Going through labeling
the skin tones

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:23.790
resulted in a smaller
gap in the errors,

00:37:23.790 --> 00:37:26.610
especially for the
medium skin tones.

00:37:26.610 --> 00:37:30.900
But there was still a problem
in errors for the darker skin

00:37:30.900 --> 00:37:33.100
tones.

00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:35.640
So here are the results
of that relabeling.

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:39.630
25% of the skin tone
labels changed categories.

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:43.110
And it completely closed
the performance gap

00:37:43.110 --> 00:37:46.060
with the medium
female skin tone.

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:51.000
So that then led us to the
additional discrepancies.

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:54.810
What we found was that
a very small subset

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:57.600
of actors and
actresses represented

00:37:57.600 --> 00:38:02.220
a significant proportion of the
total missed identifications

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:04.380
in that evaluation data set.

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:08.130
And this was especially for
the dark skin tone groups

00:38:08.130 --> 00:38:10.750
and especially for men.

00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:14.130
So with that knowledge, that
a select few actors were

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:18.180
disproportionately affecting the
error rates in this data set,

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:22.350
we looked at those actors with
the largest number of errors

00:38:22.350 --> 00:38:29.040
and found that they had nearly
a 100% false rejection rate.

00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:32.130
Because we had made
the decision earlier

00:38:32.130 --> 00:38:35.550
not to do a general
face recognition API,

00:38:35.550 --> 00:38:38.580
but to take this
carefully scoped approach,

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:42.570
we were actually
able to go one by one

00:38:42.570 --> 00:38:45.390
through the gallery and
the test set to determine

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:47.380
what the problem was.

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:49.290
So here's what we found.

00:38:49.290 --> 00:38:53.010
It was really three actors
where our celebrity gallery had

00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:56.935
images of those actors as
adults, while the Eastwood

00:38:56.935 --> 00:39:00.960
training set had videos
of them much younger all

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:03.570
from the TV show
"Family Matters."

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:07.680
Our model could not
recognize the adult actors

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:11.940
as the younger characters they
had played many years prior.

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:15.510
In this instance, for our
celebrity recognition model,

00:39:15.510 --> 00:39:19.080
we were able to correct that
problem by hand labeling, which

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:21.000
closed the gap completely.

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:23.670
And that is what allowed
us to get comfort

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:25.500
with launching this product.

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:28.590
But the answer
here is not to say

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:32.730
that this was an instance
of age in the images.

00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:37.170
Because we took the time to
look at the larger landscape,

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:40.990
and we know the issues of
representation in media,

00:39:40.990 --> 00:39:43.050
the fact that there
is a discrepancy

00:39:43.050 --> 00:39:46.650
gave us pause to think
about what might actually

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:49.510
be happening here.

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:53.620
This is an example of why
this process is so important.

00:39:53.620 --> 00:39:55.570
And these were some
of the headlines.

00:39:55.570 --> 00:39:58.660
And in fact, this is
the BSR report as well.

00:39:58.660 --> 00:40:02.260
But really, it's not
about slow walking.

00:40:02.260 --> 00:40:05.230
It's about responsible
development of AI,

00:40:05.230 --> 00:40:09.040
which is what leads to
successful deployment of AI.

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:12.310
None of this exists outside
of the larger landscape

00:40:12.310 --> 00:40:13.930
we've looked at initially.

00:40:13.930 --> 00:40:16.930
And it's because we started
with that understanding,

00:40:16.930 --> 00:40:19.100
we were prepared to address it.

00:40:19.100 --> 00:40:22.540
And we have to consider
that at every step.

00:40:22.540 --> 00:40:25.540
Ethical AI is not only
about the extremes.

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:28.730
And it's not always
about AI for social good.

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:30.100
And it shouldn't be.

00:40:30.100 --> 00:40:33.010
It's about being part
of every use case

00:40:33.010 --> 00:40:36.580
where our job is to find
a path through extremely

00:40:36.580 --> 00:40:41.940
complex and
intersecting realities.

00:40:41.940 --> 00:40:45.780
This case study will show you
how we responded to two issues

00:40:45.780 --> 00:40:49.410
with a generally available
product, which was our Google

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:53.300
Cloud Vision API.

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:55.400
Labeling is a
common practice used

00:40:55.400 --> 00:40:58.580
to classify images and train
machine learning models

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:00.350
across many applications.

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:03.440
Labeling is core to
vision AI products.

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:06.920
And in 2019, we
identified an instance

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:12.170
that violated our AI principle
to avoid creating unfair bias.

00:41:12.170 --> 00:41:15.050
Employees are our best
source of feedback.

00:41:15.050 --> 00:41:18.720
And that is how we were
able to identify this issue.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:23.150
A Googler ran an image
of themselves in the API,

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:26.700
returning a result
that misgendered them.

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:29.240
The Googler flagged this
to the Cloud Vision product

00:41:29.240 --> 00:41:34.720
team, which set forth an
investigation into this issue.

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:38.510
Through the review and
assessment of this escalation,

00:41:38.510 --> 00:41:40.360
it was important
to put this issue

00:41:40.360 --> 00:41:44.710
in the context of the user
by identifying the cause

00:41:44.710 --> 00:41:46.780
and effect this could have.

00:41:46.780 --> 00:41:50.260
Gender misidentification, like
what happened to this Googler,

00:41:50.260 --> 00:41:54.310
occurs because a person's
gender cannot be inferred based

00:41:54.310 --> 00:41:58.180
on their physical appearance
by a human or model.

00:41:58.180 --> 00:42:01.060
Their outward appearance
that the model uses

00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:05.320
is not a determining factor
when assessing gender.

00:42:05.320 --> 00:42:09.880
And the impact of doing
so exacerbates or creates

00:42:09.880 --> 00:42:13.600
unfair assumptions
that restrict or harm

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:17.650
those who do not look
stereotypically male or female,

00:42:17.650 --> 00:42:22.190
or who are a gender
nonconforming person.

00:42:22.190 --> 00:42:24.440
The steps to evaluate
an escalation

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:25.820
are not always the same.

00:42:25.820 --> 00:42:29.150
Some require in-depth product
testing, industry expertise,

00:42:29.150 --> 00:42:31.910
third party
assessments, and so on.

00:42:31.910 --> 00:42:35.540
Having identified the issue
that potentially harmful impacts

00:42:35.540 --> 00:42:38.750
users, we formulated
a go-forward plan.

00:42:38.750 --> 00:42:42.020
Reinforcing Google's commitment
to build products for everyone,

00:42:42.020 --> 00:42:45.230
we made the decision that
a person's gender cannot be

00:42:45.230 --> 00:42:46.540
inferred by appearance.

00:42:46.540 --> 00:42:50.090
And attempting to do so
could perpetuate unfair bias.

00:42:50.090 --> 00:42:54.500
Removing man and woman labels
from the Cloud Vision API

00:42:54.500 --> 00:42:57.440
and replacing them with
a non-gender label,

00:42:57.440 --> 00:43:00.830
such as person, is
the act we took.

00:43:03.393 --> 00:43:05.560
Through this process, we
learned a couple key things

00:43:05.560 --> 00:43:08.960
that altered our approach
to gendered label data.

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:10.990
There are often more
targeted signals

00:43:10.990 --> 00:43:13.720
to attribute to a person
that provide more valuable

00:43:13.720 --> 00:43:15.790
information to a customer.

00:43:15.790 --> 00:43:18.690
Taking retail stores
as an example,

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:20.980
we might historically
analyzed foot traffic

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:23.020
with demographic information.

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:26.650
A more targeted labeling could
be a person's fashion sense

00:43:26.650 --> 00:43:29.510
to analyze foot traffic
and cater their inventory

00:43:29.510 --> 00:43:31.550
to those fashion types.

00:43:31.550 --> 00:43:33.640
We also came to the
conclusion that,

00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:37.510
when it comes to general tools
where we do not have control

00:43:37.510 --> 00:43:41.860
over customer use cases,
to take a cautious approach

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:44.230
with capabilities
and functionality,

00:43:44.230 --> 00:43:46.480
and build targeted
solutions where

00:43:46.480 --> 00:43:51.770
we have a hand in the
development and use case.

00:43:51.770 --> 00:43:55.340
The second example is the result
of an escalation of our Vision

00:43:55.340 --> 00:43:57.860
API earlier this year, 2020.

00:43:57.860 --> 00:44:00.890
And it shows how
important an understanding

00:44:00.890 --> 00:44:05.840
of the societal context is
in which our products exist.

00:44:05.840 --> 00:44:07.670
Here is the escalation.

00:44:07.670 --> 00:44:10.820
You can see here, the
image with a Black person

00:44:10.820 --> 00:44:14.700
and a handheld thermometer
returned a label for firearm.

00:44:14.700 --> 00:44:18.200
And an Asian person holding
a handheld thermometer

00:44:18.200 --> 00:44:21.775
returned a label for
electronic devices.

00:44:21.775 --> 00:44:23.150
This was brought
to our attention

00:44:23.150 --> 00:44:26.840
through the tweet you
see here on the left.

00:44:26.840 --> 00:44:29.240
What happened next
was someone taking

00:44:29.240 --> 00:44:32.450
a photo of the Black man
holding a thermometer gun

00:44:32.450 --> 00:44:37.040
and manipulating to make the
skin appear to be lighter.

00:44:37.040 --> 00:44:42.110
As you can see, this changed
the label from gun to tool,

00:44:42.110 --> 00:44:46.400
which is a deeply
distressing result.

00:44:46.400 --> 00:44:50.760
It is critical to recognize
why a result like this

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:52.680
can create increased harm.

00:44:52.680 --> 00:44:55.560
The statistics here highlight
some of the societal context

00:44:55.560 --> 00:44:57.090
in which this lives.

00:44:57.090 --> 00:45:01.800
Any robust evaluation of this
escalation cannot separate this

00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:04.920
reality of racial
justice, racial violence,

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:09.570
and systemic racism from
the technical analysis.

00:45:09.570 --> 00:45:11.090
So what did we do?

00:45:11.090 --> 00:45:14.000
Our investigations led
us across multiple teams.

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:16.820
And you can see here the
results of some of those.

00:45:16.820 --> 00:45:19.130
Changing the skin tone
in the first example,

00:45:19.130 --> 00:45:21.530
through some of our
counterfactual testing,

00:45:21.530 --> 00:45:23.870
does change the
API's prediction.

00:45:23.870 --> 00:45:27.740
But what we found was that
gun was still a top 5 label

00:45:27.740 --> 00:45:30.890
response for all three
images in skin tones,

00:45:30.890 --> 00:45:34.790
although API demo didn't
surface this result.

00:45:34.790 --> 00:45:39.260
We used our own explanations
AI to look at saliency mapping.

00:45:39.260 --> 00:45:42.050
Saliency maps for
the third hand images

00:45:42.050 --> 00:45:44.960
show that the attributes
cluster predominantly

00:45:44.960 --> 00:45:47.570
on the thermometer
for all three images

00:45:47.570 --> 00:45:49.940
with some bleed into
the upper fingers

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:54.410
of the hand for the darker
and medium skin tone hands.

00:45:54.410 --> 00:45:57.650
When we went into our
counterfactual testing further,

00:45:57.650 --> 00:46:00.710
you could see that a change
of color of the thermometer

00:46:00.710 --> 00:46:04.430
gun in the original
image also caused the gun

00:46:04.430 --> 00:46:06.840
label to disappear.

00:46:06.840 --> 00:46:08.970
We then did stability testing.

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:11.990
And in this case, the
image appears very

00:46:11.990 --> 00:46:13.880
sensitive to image cropping.

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:18.710
Even minor variations make
that gun label disappear.

00:46:18.710 --> 00:46:21.110
We also looked at
our training data.

00:46:21.110 --> 00:46:25.760
We don't have many images of
handheld infrared thermometers.

00:46:25.760 --> 00:46:29.660
And so this was a gap, as it's
a newer product in our own image

00:46:29.660 --> 00:46:31.220
database.

00:46:31.220 --> 00:46:33.830
And it's not
necessarily incorrect

00:46:33.830 --> 00:46:35.840
that this be
labeled gun, as they

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:38.030
are called thermometer guns.

00:46:38.030 --> 00:46:40.160
But if that's the
case, then they

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:43.010
should be labeled
that way unilaterally.

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:46.610
We found that many images
were mislabeled gun,

00:46:46.610 --> 00:46:50.330
including things like
cameras, for example.

00:46:50.330 --> 00:46:54.710
This example represents an
all hands on deck process

00:46:54.710 --> 00:46:58.130
that required a humble
approach and thoughtful action

00:46:58.130 --> 00:46:59.210
to remedy.

00:46:59.210 --> 00:47:01.820
By conducting a detailed
assessment of the root

00:47:01.820 --> 00:47:05.360
cause of this issue, we
were able to understand

00:47:05.360 --> 00:47:08.960
that model accuracy and
stability were key drivers.

00:47:08.960 --> 00:47:11.390
And by adjusting the
confidence threshold

00:47:11.390 --> 00:47:15.140
to more precisely return labels
when a firearm is present,

00:47:15.140 --> 00:47:18.380
we could greatly decrease
the chance of this happening

00:47:18.380 --> 00:47:19.910
in the future.

00:47:19.910 --> 00:47:22.640
This example showed
us, again, that we

00:47:22.640 --> 00:47:27.830
have to do the work to put
the harm in context in order

00:47:27.830 --> 00:47:31.100
to truly assess
the extent of it.

00:47:31.100 --> 00:47:36.920
It isn't enough to say that this
was only about model accuracy

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:40.940
and stability because
this result was harmful.

00:47:40.940 --> 00:47:44.990
And it exists in a world
of much larger harms

00:47:44.990 --> 00:47:49.280
that we have to think about
when we address a problem.

00:47:49.280 --> 00:47:51.290
What this taught us
through experience

00:47:51.290 --> 00:47:53.720
was that none of
the evaluations we

00:47:53.720 --> 00:47:58.610
do exist outside of the large
landscape and that context

00:47:58.610 --> 00:47:59.960
in which they lived.

00:47:59.960 --> 00:48:03.800
And that's the case for
every analysis we undertake,

00:48:03.800 --> 00:48:07.460
for every product we launch,
for every decision we

00:48:07.460 --> 00:48:12.740
make about how to best respect
our users, the opportunities,

00:48:12.740 --> 00:48:15.080
and each other.

00:48:15.080 --> 00:48:17.670
Thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:48:17.670 --> 00:48:20.480
I hope you've enjoyed
this conversation.

00:48:20.480 --> 00:48:25.030
And I look forward to
many more in the future.

